# The interface's configuration, in full. Copy to `.env.local` and edit.
#
# Every variable here is `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, which means Next substitutes its value into the
# browser bundle at build time. That is correct for all of them — a chain id, an RPC and
# a set of contract addresses are public facts about a deployment, and an interface that
# kept them secret would be one nobody could verify. Do not add a secret to this file.
#
# Unset everything and the app targets Robinhood mainnet and reads the deployment recorded
# in `packages/config/src/deployments.ts`, which is the live one. So the overrides below are
# for pointing this app somewhere else — a local rig, mostly — rather than for making it
# work.

# --- which chain ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# Defaults to 4663 (Robinhood mainnet). 46630 is its testnet. A local anvil rig usually
# also claims 4663, which is why the RPC is a separate setting.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CHAIN_ID=4663

# Defaults to the chain's public endpoint. Set it for a local rig, or to use your own
# node rather than Robinhood's.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8545

# --- where Verdant is ----------------------------------------------------------------

# These override `DEPLOYMENTS`, which records mainnet and nothing else, so they are what a
# rig needs and what mainnet does not. `script/Deploy.s.sol` prints exactly these; a rig
# prints its own.
#
# A missing or malformed address is reported in the interface, by name, at the point a
# transaction would have been offered. Nothing is ever sent to an address the app does
# not have.

# NEXT_PUBLIC_VERDANT_FACTORY=0x…
# NEXT_PUBLIC_VERDANT_HOOK=0x…
# NEXT_PUBLIC_VERDANT_DEPLOYER=0x…
# NEXT_PUBLIC_VERDANT_MODEL_REGISTRY=0x…
# NEXT_PUBLIC_VERDANT_MARKET_REGISTRY=0x…

# --- where uploaded images go --------------------------------------------------------

# The one secret this app takes, and the only variable here that is not `NEXT_PUBLIC_`:
# it is used by `/api/image` on the server and never reaches the browser.
#
# Set, and an uploaded token picture is stored durably and the token records the public
# address it landed at. Unset, uploads still work — the control has to be usable in a fresh
# clone — but they are written to `.uploads/` and served from this origin, which the launch
# form recognises as an address only this machine can answer and refuses to record in a
# token. So a clone can use the whole form; it cannot accidentally launch a market whose
# picture never loads.
#
# `vercel blob create-store <name> --access public --yes`, run from this directory with the
# project linked, creates the store and writes this into `.env.local`.
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=vercel_blob_rw_…

# --- where the listings come from ----------------------------------------------------

# The indexer. Server-side only: the pages that read it are server components, so this is
# deliberately not a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` variable and the browser never sees it.
#
# The deployed feed follows mainnet and is public, so pointing a local app at it is the
# easy way to see real markets without running an indexer of your own — and the way to
# avoid the failure that costs an afternoon, which is two Ponder processes sharing one
# PGlite directory. See `railway.toml`.
# VERDANT_FEED_URL=https://indexer-production-2c72.up.railway.app
# VERDANT_FEED_URL=http://127.0.0.1:42069
